# Copy to deploy/.env and replace every value before running docker compose.
# CLUCKWORK_HOST is both the traefik router host AND the pinned AllowedHosts
# value (#144): requests with any other Host header are rejected 400. Loopback
# stays allowed so the container healthcheck works.
CLUCKWORK_HOST=cluckwork.local
POSTGRES_DB=cluckwork
POSTGRES_USER=cluckwork
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-strong-password
# Database connection (#261/#262). docker-compose.yml builds ConnectionStrings__Default
# from the POSTGRES_* values above. In PRODUCTION the app enforces a TLS floor and
# FAILS TO BOOT on a connection that does not guarantee TLS: a production connection
# string MUST set sslmode=Require at minimum (sslmode=VerifyFull with a host CA is
# strongly preferred; sslmode=Require only warns; VerifyCA/VerifyFull are silent).
# URI-form strings are also accepted now — e.g.
# ConnectionStrings__Default=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/db?sslmode=require
# (both postgres:// and postgresql:// schemes; the password is URL-decoded; unknown
# libpq params such as channel_binding are ignored-with-warning).
#
# Opt-out for a co-located plaintext DB: Database__AllowInsecureConnection=true downgrades
# the boot failure to a LOUD warning and boots anyway. The bundled compose sets it on the
# app + migrate services (single host, db never published — plaintext over the private
# bridge is acceptable there). A real deploy uses TLS/managed Postgres and NEVER sets it.
# Database__AllowInsecureConnection=false
Jwt__Issuer=cluckwork
Jwt__Audience=cluckwork-api
# BOTH keys are checked at boot, and the SERVING process refuses to start unless
# each is present and actually parses (#510). The armor below is a placeholder,
# not a key — copy this file unchanged and the API will tell you so and exit,
# rather than booting green, passing /health/ready, and 500ing every login. Keep
# the surrounding quotes and the escaped \n; that is the format PemKey.Normalize
# expects. The one-shot verbs (migrate / seed / recover-admin / bootstrap-admin /
# list-accounts / suspend-account / reactivate-account / provision-account /
# healthcheck) never need these — they neither issue nor validate a token (#347).
Jwt__PublicKeyPem="-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\nreplace-me\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----"
Jwt__PrivateKeyPem="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nreplace-me\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----"

# Base provisioning (#283): the default account, the four assignable roles,
# and the default egg grades ship AS PART OF the EF migrations (static
# reference data — no Seed:* config, no runtime seeder). A freshly migrated
# database is already usable; there is nothing to set here for it.
#
# The first admin is NOT baked in (that would ship every deployment the same
# repo-known credential). After the stack is up, run the one-shot
# `bootstrap-admin` command once against this database:
#   docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.yml run --rm app \
#     bootstrap-admin --email admin@example.com
# Pass the VERB ONLY: the image's ENTRYPOINT is already
# `dotnet Cluckwork.Api.dll` and `docker compose run` APPENDS to it, so
# repeating it would make args[0] be `dotnet`, match no verb, and boot the web
# server instead of provisioning (same reason the `migrate` service below is
# `command: ["migrate"]`).
# It generates a random password, creates the Owner with it, and writes the
# password to stdout only — never to the application logger or telemetry
# (OTLP). A host's stdout collector (docker logs, journald, a platform log
# pipeline) may still capture it, so treat that output as sensitive while the
# password is valid. Sign in with it; the app immediately requires setting a
# new password before anything else works. Re-running the command against an
# already-provisioned account is a safe no-op.
#
# Set to false to skip applying EF migrations on startup.
# Database__MigrateOnStartup=true

# Demo sample data (#280/#284) is command-only — there is no boot-time toggle
# here (no Seed__Demo). Run it explicitly against an already-provisioned,
# non-Production database: `dotnet Cluckwork.Api.dll seed --profile demo`.

# Auth rate limiting (#143): login and refresh are throttled separately per
# client IP (login strict, refresh looser so normal session traffic behind one
# NAT doesn't starve login). The client IP is read from X-Forwarded-For only for
# requests arriving from the trusted proxy network below (traefik's compose
# network; CIDR notation, /32 for a single address).
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR=172.16.0.0/12
# #260: an empty trusted-proxy list in Production fails the boot (HSTS #144 goes
# inert and the per-IP login limiter #143 collapses to one bucket). Set true ONLY
# for a direct-TLS-exposure deploy with no fronting proxy. Default false.
# RateLimiting__AllowNoTrustedProxies=false
# RateLimiting__Login__PermitLimit=10
# RateLimiting__Login__WindowSeconds=900
# RateLimiting__Refresh__PermitLimit=60
# RateLimiting__Refresh__WindowSeconds=900

# Browser error reports (#217): the anonymous /api/v1/client-errors endpoint is
# throttled per client IP so it can't be used to flood the log. The default is
# enough for a genuinely crashing screen, tiny for an abuser.
# RateLimiting__ClientErrors__PermitLimit=10
# RateLimiting__ClientErrors__WindowSeconds=300

# OpenTelemetry export (#214 traces, #215 metrics). Both signals go over OTLP
# to the SAME endpoint, ONLY when it is set; leave it unset to keep telemetry
# in-process (no exporter, no overhead). A malformed endpoint or protocol
# fails at startup — fix the value, don't ignore the crash loop.
# Traces: HTTP requests + EF Core queries (health probes excluded).
# Metrics (meter set): ALL built-in ASP.NET Core meters — hosting
# (http.server.request.duration), Kestrel, routing, rate limiting,
# authentication/authorization/Identity, memory pool — plus .NET runtime
# (GC/threads/exceptions), Npgsql (connections/commands), and EF Core
# (Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore). All low-cardinality; request-duration
# histograms INCLUDE health probes (the metrics side has no per-request
# filter). Temporality (cumulative) and the 60s export interval are SDK
# defaults — override per vendor via OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_METRICS_TEMPORALITY_PREFERENCE
# and OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_INTERVAL, no code change needed.
# Protocol is 'grpc' (default, usually port 4317) or 'http/protobuf' (usually
# 4318 — most SaaS vendors). For http/protobuf give the BASE url (no /v1/traces
# — the app appends the signal path itself). Headers carry vendor auth,
# comma-separated key=value pairs — NEVER put credentials in Endpoint itself
# (#316): userinfo, a query string, and a fragment on Otlp:Endpoint all fail
# validation in every environment, and Production additionally requires
# https (plaintext HTTP exposes telemetry and the Headers credentials in
# transit). The one escape hatch is Otlp__AllowInsecureEndpoint=true, for a
# collector that is a private peer the traffic never leaves: a loopback
# collector on a dev box, or a sidecar on the same private compose network
# (the #243 sim stack sets it for exactly that). It is the same posture as
# Database__AllowInsecureConnection, and carries the same warning — it DOES
# permit plaintext to any host, so a real deploy never sets it.
# Otlp__Endpoint=https://otlp.example.com:4317
# Otlp__Protocol=grpc
# Otlp__Headers=Authorization=Basic replace-me
# Otlp__AllowInsecureEndpoint=true
